2026-06-30 15:43:28,907 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-7854206b-ca59-415f-a44e-dc4412f3805e', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert proteomics assistant. Return valid JSON only.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'You are a proteomics expert analyzing crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nYour task is to cluster raw spectral files into biologically meaningful groups.\n\nA cluster should represent a unique combination of:\n1. Biological / experimental condition\n   - Examples: treatment, control, carbon source, growth medium, cell state, tissue, disease state, time point, perturbation, purified complex, recombinant standard set.\n2. Source organism of the proteome being analyzed\n   - Return the full scientific name.\n   - Use the organism of the biological sample or proteome of interest, not necessarily the expression host.\n   - Example: if human proteins are expressed in E. coli and analyzed as human recombinant standards, the source organism is Homo sapiens.\n   - If the dataset intentionally contains multiple source organisms, create separate clusters by organism when possible.\n3. Crosslinking reagent\n   - Examples: BS3, DSS, DSSO, DSBU, formaldehyde.\n   - If multiple crosslinkers are used, create separate clusters by reagent when possible.\n\nDo NOT split clusters by:\n- fraction number\n- LC fraction\n- technical replicate\n- biological replicate ID\n- injection number\n- file numbering\n- enrichment fraction\n- instrument run order\n- search batch\n- calibration/QC files\n\nReplicates, fractions, and repeated injections from the same biological condition should be grouped together.\n\n---\n\nINPUTS\n\nRaw filenames:\nE9890_MS1_Nctrl1-0.raw\nE9891_MS2_Nctrl1-1.raw\nE9892_MS3_Nctrl1-2.raw\nE9893_MS4_Nctrl1-5.raw\nE9894_MS5_Nctrl2-0.raw\nE9895_MS6_Nctrl2-1.raw\nE9896_MS7_Nctrl2-2.raw\nE9897_MS8_Nctrl2-5.raw\nE9898_MS9_Nctrl3-0.raw\nE9899_MS10_Nctrl3-1.raw\nE9900_MS11_Nctrl3-2.raw\nE9901_MS12_Nctrl3-5.raw\nE9902_MS13_NTNF1-0.raw\nE9903_MS14_NTNF1-1.raw\nE9904_MS15_NTNF1-2.raw\nE9905_MS16_NTNF1-5.raw\nE9906_MS17_NTNF2-0.raw\nE9907_MS18_NTNF2-1.raw\nE9908_MS19_NTNF2-2.raw\nE9909_MS20_NTNF2-5.raw\nE9910_MS21_NTNF3-0.raw\nE9911_MS22_NTNF3-1.raw\nE9912_MS23_NTNF3-2.raw\nE9913_MS24_NTNF3-5.raw\nE9914_MS25_Hctrl1-0.raw\nE9915_MS26_Hctrl1-1.raw\nE9916_MS27_Hctrl1-2.raw\nE9917_MS28_Hctrl1-5.raw\nE9918_MS29_Hctrl2-0.raw\nE9919_MS30_Hctrl2-1.raw\nE9920_MS31_Hctrl2-2.raw\nE9921_MS32_Hctrl2-5.raw\nE9922_MS33_Hctrl3-0.raw\nE9923_MS34_Hctrl3-1.raw\nE9924_MS35_Hctrl3-2.raw\nE9925_MS36_Hctrl3-5.raw\nE9926_MS37_HTNF1-0.raw\nE9927_MS38_HTNF1-1.raw\nE9928_MS39_HTNF1-2.raw\nE9929_MS40_HTNF1-5.raw\nE9930_MS41_HTNF2-0.raw\nE9931_MS42_HTNF2-1.raw\nE9932_MS43_HTNF2-2.raw\nE9933_MS44_HTNF2-5.raw\nE9934_MS45_HTNF3-0.raw\nE9935_MS46_HTNF3-1.raw\nE9936_MS47_HTNF3-2.raw\nE9937_MS48_HTNF3-5.raw\n\nPublication text and PRIDE metadata:\n[TITLE]\nDynamic Changes in the Proximitome of Neutral Sphingomyelinase-2 (NSM2) in TNFα Stimulated Jurkat Cells\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nCeramides generated by the activity of the neutral sphingomyelinase 2 (NSM2) play a pivotal role in stress responses in mammalian cells. Dysregulation of sphingolipid metabolism has been implicated in numerous inflammation-related pathologies. However, its influence on inflammatory cytokine-induced signaling is yet incompletely understood. Here, we used proximity labeling to explore the plasma membrane proximal protein network of NSM2 and TNFα-induced changes thereof. We established Jurkat cells stably expressing NSM2 C-terminally fused to the engineered ascorbate peroxidase 2 (APEX2). Removal of excess biotin phenol substantially improved streptavidin-based affinity purification of biotinylated proteins. Using our optimized protocol, we determined NSM2-proximal biotinylated proteins and their changes within the first 5 min of TNFα stimulation by quantitative mass spectrometry. We observed significant dynamic changes in the NSM2 microenvironment in response to TNFα\uf020stimulation consistent with rapid remodeling of protein networks. Our data confirmed known NSM2 interactors and revealed that the recruitment of most proteins depended on NSM2 enzymatic activity. We measured significant enrichment of proteins related to vesicle-mediated transport, including proteins of recycling endosomes, trans-Golgi network, and exocytic vesicles in the proximitome of enzymatically active NSM2 within the first minutes of TNFα stimulation. Hence, the NSM2 proximal network and its TNFα-induced changes provide a valuable resource for further investigations into the involvement of NSM2 in the early signaling pathways triggered by TNFα.\n\n[METHODS]\nAPEX2 proximity labeling was performed according to the published protocol from Hung et al. with optimization for Jurkat cells. Briefly, 1.5x107 Jurkat cells stably expressing NSM2-APEX2 were incubated in 30 mL 10% RPMI containing 1 mM BP (Iris Biotech) at 37°C on a rotary wheel for 30 min.; cells were pelleted for 5 min at 457 g and resuspended in 3 mL 10% RPMI for stimulation and labeling reaction. 100 ng/mL TNFα (Thermo Fisher) was added, and cells were incubated at 37°C using a water bath for the indicated time points (1, 2, and 5 min). To initiate the labeling, H2O2 (30% (wt/wt), Sigma-Aldrich) was added to a final concentration of 1 mM for the final 1 min of stimulation. For the respective negative control, labeling was initiated as described, but TNFα was omitted. The APEX2 labeling reaction was quenched by the addition of 20 mL ice-cold quenching solution (10 mM ascorbate, 5 mM Trolox, and 10 mM sodium azide in PBS) to the cell suspension. Cells were pelleted immediately (1 min at 2862 g, 4°C), and the supernatant was discarded. Cells were washed 3x with a quenching solution and resuspended in 1.5 mL of ice-cold PBS. 5x106 cells (~ 500 μL) were separated and used to validate TNFα signaling activation and successful biotinylation by western blot. The remaining sample was pelleted and lysed in 100 μL RIPA buffer (50 mM Tris-HCl (pH 7.5), 150 mM NaCl, 1% (vol/vol) Triton X-100, 0.1% SDS, 0.5% deoxycholate, 1 mM PMSF, Protease Inhibitor Cocktail (Thermo Fisher Scientific)) supplemented with quenchers (10 mM sodium azide, 10 mM sodium ascorbate and 5 mM Trolox) for 20 min on ice. To ensure maximum recovery of biotinylated proteins, excess BP was removed by filtering the post-nuclear lysates using 3K MWCO protein concentrator columns (Pierce, Thermo Fisher Scientific). Biotinylated proteins were then enriched using 20 µL streptavidin magnetic beads (Pierce, Thermo Fisher Scientific) per sample at 4°C overnight. The bead-bound proteins were then washed with a series of buffers (2x RIPA, 1x 1 M KCl, 1x 0.1 M Na2CO3, 1x 2 M urea in 10 mM Tris-HCl, pH 8.0, 2x RIPA) and either subjected to down-stream MS or analyzed by western blot. Streptavidin magnetic beads containing bead-bound proteins were washed and stored in 100 µL 100 mM Triethylammoniumbicarbonat buffer (TEAB). For that, proteins were digested on-bead using trypsin. Proteins were reduced for 1 hour at 55°C using a final concentration of 5 mM Tris(2-carboxyethyl) phosphin (TCEP) followed by reduction for 30 min at room temperature using a final concentration of 10 mM methyl methanethiosulfonate (MMTS). Trypsin was added in a ratio of 1 µg trypsin to 30 µg protein, and proteins were digested overnight at 37°C while shaking. Beads were spun down, and supernatants were transferred to LowBind Eppendorf tubes. Beads were resuspended in 50 µL 250 mM TEAB, spun down, and supernatants were combined and vacuum dried. Pellets were suspended in 50 µL 250 mM TEAB containing 5 mM TCEP and 10 mM MMTS and sonicated for 10 min in an ultrasound water bath. For peptide clean-up, an adapted SP3 protocol was applied. For that, 20 µL carboxylate beads were added, and peptides were allowed to bind overnight upon acetonitrile (ACN) addition to a final concentration of at least 95%. Beads were spun down, supernatants were transferred to new LowBind Eppendorf tubes, and 20 µL carboxylate beads were added to increase peptide recovery. Then, beads were combined and washed twice with 100% ACN. Peptides were eluted by using first 20 µL 2% DMSO, followed by a second step with 20 µL Millipore H2O. Purified peptides were vacuum dried, and pellets were suspended in 40 µL 0.1% formic acid (FA) and transferred to HPLC vials.\n\n\nPreviously extracted organism(s) and NCBI taxid(s):\nHomo sapiens (human)\n\n---\n\nTASKS\n\n1. Identify the distinct biological/experimental conditions described by the publication, PRIDE metadata, and filenames.\n\n2. Assign every raw file to exactly one cluster.\n\n3. Define clusters using the smallest number of biologically meaningful groups. Do not over-cluster based only on replicate, fraction, or run-number differences.\n\n4. For each cluster, report:\n   - cluster_id: "cluster_1", "cluster_2", etc.\n   - cluster_label: short machine-readable label using lowercase words separated by underscores\n   - description: concise human-readable summary of the condition\n   - organism: full scientific name of the proteome/sample organism\n   - taxid: NCBI Taxonomy CURIE, e.g. "NCBITaxon:9606"; use null if unknown\n   - crosslinker: crosslinking reagent; use null if unknown\n   - biological_condition: short phrase describing the treatment/control/state\n   - files: list of raw filenames assigned to this cluster\n   - confidence: "high", "medium", or "low"\n   - evidence_quote: exact sentence or short phrase from the publication, PRIDE metadata, or filename pattern that supports this cluster assignment\n   - reasoning: brief explanation of why these files belong together\n\n5. Assign files that cannot be confidently interpreted to cluster_0 with:\n   - cluster_label: "unassigned"\n   - confidence: "low"\n   - evidence_quote: null\n   - reasoning: explain why assignment was not possible\n\n6. QC, calibrant, blank, wash, or test files should be assigned to cluster_0 unless the publication clearly states they are part of the biological experiment.\n\n7. If all biological files appear to come from one condition, create only cluster_1 plus cluster_0 if there are unassigned/QC files.\n\n8. Confidence guidelines:\n   - "high": filenames and publication/metadata agree clearly\n   - "medium": assignment is likely but partially inferred from filenames or incomplete metadata\n   - "low": weak evidence, ambiguous naming, missing metadata, or conflicting information\n\n9. If publication text and filenames conflict, prioritize the publication/PRIDE metadata, but mention the conflict in reasoning.\n\n10. Return ONLY valid JSON. Do not include markdown, comments, or explanatory text outside the JSON.\n\n---\n\nOUTPUT JSON STRUCTURE\n\n{\n  "clusters": [\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_1",\n      "cluster_label": "yeast_glucose_bs3",\n      "description": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae samples grown in glucose-rich medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "organism": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae",\n      "taxid": "NCBITaxon:4932",\n      "crosslinker": "BS3",\n      "biological_condition": "glucose-rich medium",\n      "files": [\n        "YeastGlu_R1.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_R2.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_fraction_01.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "high",\n      "evidence_quote": "Yeast cells were cultured in glucose- or glycerol-containing medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "reasoning": "The filenames contain Glu and the publication states that glucose-grown yeast samples were crosslinked with BS3."\n    },\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_0",\n      "cluster_label": "unassigned",\n      "description": "Files that could not be confidently assigned to a biological XL-MS condition.",\n      "organism": null,\n      "taxid": null,\n      "crosslinker": null,\n      "biological_condition": null,\n      "files": [\n        "calibrant.raw",\n        "QC.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "low",\n      "evidence_quote": null,\n      "reasoning": "These files appear to be QC or calibration runs rather than biological samples."\n    }\n  ],\n  "summary": {\n    "n_input_files": 5,\n    "n_assigned_files": 3,\n    "n_unassigned_files": 2,\n    "n_biological_clusters": 1,\n    "overall_confidence": "high"\n  }\n}'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
