2026-06-30 15:43:48,820 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-94e4f6bc-f5b7-4eaf-8343-6966fbd238cf', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert SDRF-Proteomics annotator for crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nExtract the following SDRF metadata terms related to the mass spectrometry acquisition.\n\nTERMS TO EXTRACT:\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nterm: instrument\nsdrf_column: comment[instrument]\nontology: MS:1000031 (PSI-MS)\ndescription: Mass spectrometer instrument make and model.\ninstructions: Use PSI-MS ontology. If multiple instruments, return a list.\n  Common instruments (name → accession):\n    Orbitrap Astral → MS:1003356\n    Orbitrap Exploris 480 → MS:1003028\n    Orbitrap Eclipse → MS:1003349\n    Orbitrap Fusion Lumos → MS:1002732\n    Orbitrap Fusion → MS:1002416\n    Q Exactive HF-X → MS:1002877\n    Q Exactive HF → MS:1002523\n    Q Exactive Plus → MS:1002634\n    Q Exactive → MS:1001911\n    LTQ Orbitrap Velos → MS:1001742\n    LTQ Orbitrap XL → MS:1000556\n    timsTOF → MS:1003005\n    timsTOF Pro → MS:1003230\n    TripleTOF 6600 → MS:1002583\n  Format: NT=<instrument name>;AC=<MS accession>\n\nterm: proteomics data acquisition method\nsdrf_column: comment[proteomics data acquisition method]\nontology: PRIDE:0000659\ndescription: Mass spectrometry data acquisition method.\ninstructions: For XL-MS this is nearly always DDA. Use:\n  NT=data-dependent acquisition;AC=PRIDE:0000041 — for DDA/shotgun\n  NT=data-independent acquisition;AC=PRIDE:0000449 — for DIA (rare in XL-MS)\n  NT=targeted acquisition;AC=PRIDE:0000450 — for SRM/PRM\n  Default to DDA if not stated.\n\nterm: dissociation method\nsdrf_column: comment[dissociation method]\nontology: MS:1000044 (PSI-MS children)\ndescription: Fragmentation method used for MS/MS.\ninstructions: Use PSI-MS terms. If multiple methods, return a list.\n  Common methods:\n    HCD → NT=HCD;AC=MS:1000422\n    CID → NT=CID;AC=MS:1002472\n    ETD → NT=ETD;AC=MS:1000598\n    EThcD → NT=EThcD;AC=MS:1002678\n    UVPD → NT=UVPD;AC=MS:1003247\n    stepped HCD → NT=HCD;AC=MS:1000422 (stepped HCD is still HCD)\n\nterm: collision energy\nsdrf_column: comment[collision energy]\nontology: MS:1000045\ndescription: Collision energy used for fragmentation.\npattern: {value} {unit} where unit is NCE, %, or eV.\n  Stepped HCD: {value} {unit};{value} {unit};{value} {unit}\n  Examples: "30 NCE", "30% NCE", "27 eV", "25 NCE;27 NCE;30 NCE"\ninstructions: Return null if not stated.\n\nterm: dia method\nsdrf_column: comment[dia method]\nontology: PRIDE:0000450\ndescription: Specific DIA method variant (only relevant for DIA datasets).\ninstructions: Return null for DDA XL-MS datasets (which is nearly all XL-MS).\n  If DIA is used, specify the variant (e.g. "SWATH", "MSe", "diaPASEF").\n\nterm: fragment mass tolerance\nsdrf_column: comment[fragment mass tolerance]\nontology: PRIDE:0000576\ndescription: Fragment ion mass tolerance for the database search.\npattern: number + ppm or Da (e.g. "20 ppm", "0.02 Da")\ninstructions: Return null if not stated. Typical XL-MS values: 10–20 ppm for\n  Orbitrap instruments, 0.02–0.05 Da for ion trap.\n\nterm: precursor mass tolerance\nsdrf_column: comment[precursor mass tolerance]\nontology: PRIDE:0000575\ndescription: Precursor ion mass tolerance for the database search.\npattern: number + ppm or Da (e.g. "10 ppm", "5 ppm")\ninstructions: Return null if not stated. Typical: 5–10 ppm.\n\nterm: technical replicate\nsdrf_column: comment[technical replicate]\nontology: MS:1001808\ndescription: Identifier for the technical replicate (integer).\ninstructions: Describe the technical replication strategy found in the text\n  (e.g. "2 technical replicates per sample", "no technical replicates stated").\n  Actual integers are assigned per-file automatically.\n\nterm: data file\nsdrf_column: comment[data file]\nontology: MS:1003083\ndescription: Name of the raw data file (.raw, .wiff, .d).\ninstructions: This is always auto-derived from the PRIDE file list.\n  Describe the file naming convention you observe (e.g. "sample_condition_rep.raw").\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nReturn a single JSON object where each key is the exact sdrf_column string shown above.\nFor each key, return a dict with:\n  "value": the extracted value (string, list, or null)\n  "accession": ontology accession if resolvable, else null\n  "confidence": "high", "medium", or "low"\n  "evidence_quote": short verbatim quote from the text, or null\n  "evidence_location": location in the paper (e.g. "Methods"), or null'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '[TITLE]\nDynamic Changes in the Proximitome of Neutral Sphingomyelinase-2 (NSM2) in TNFα Stimulated Jurkat Cells\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nCeramides generated by the activity of the neutral sphingomyelinase 2 (NSM2) play a pivotal role in stress responses in mammalian cells. Dysregulation of sphingolipid metabolism has been implicated in numerous inflammation-related pathologies. However, its influence on inflammatory cytokine-induced signaling is yet incompletely understood. Here, we used proximity labeling to explore the plasma membrane proximal protein network of NSM2 and TNFα-induced changes thereof. We established Jurkat cells stably expressing NSM2 C-terminally fused to the engineered ascorbate peroxidase 2 (APEX2). Removal of excess biotin phenol substantially improved streptavidin-based affinity purification of biotinylated proteins. Using our optimized protocol, we determined NSM2-proximal biotinylated proteins and their changes within the first 5 min of TNFα stimulation by quantitative mass spectrometry. We observed significant dynamic changes in the NSM2 microenvironment in response to TNFα\uf020stimulation consistent with rapid remodeling of protein networks. Our data confirmed known NSM2 interactors and revealed that the recruitment of most proteins depended on NSM2 enzymatic activity. We measured significant enrichment of proteins related to vesicle-mediated transport, including proteins of recycling endosomes, trans-Golgi network, and exocytic vesicles in the proximitome of enzymatically active NSM2 within the first minutes of TNFα stimulation. Hence, the NSM2 proximal network and its TNFα-induced changes provide a valuable resource for further investigations into the involvement of NSM2 in the early signaling pathways triggered by TNFα.\n\n[METHODS]\nAPEX2 proximity labeling was performed according to the published protocol from Hung et al. with optimization for Jurkat cells. Briefly, 1.5x107 Jurkat cells stably expressing NSM2-APEX2 were incubated in 30 mL 10% RPMI containing 1 mM BP (Iris Biotech) at 37°C on a rotary wheel for 30 min.; cells were pelleted for 5 min at 457 g and resuspended in 3 mL 10% RPMI for stimulation and labeling reaction. 100 ng/mL TNFα (Thermo Fisher) was added, and cells were incubated at 37°C using a water bath for the indicated time points (1, 2, and 5 min). To initiate the labeling, H2O2 (30% (wt/wt), Sigma-Aldrich) was added to a final concentration of 1 mM for the final 1 min of stimulation. For the respective negative control, labeling was initiated as described, but TNFα was omitted. The APEX2 labeling reaction was quenched by the addition of 20 mL ice-cold quenching solution (10 mM ascorbate, 5 mM Trolox, and 10 mM sodium azide in PBS) to the cell suspension. Cells were pelleted immediately (1 min at 2862 g, 4°C), and the supernatant was discarded. Cells were washed 3x with a quenching solution and resuspended in 1.5 mL of ice-cold PBS. 5x106 cells (~ 500 μL) were separated and used to validate TNFα signaling activation and successful biotinylation by western blot. The remaining sample was pelleted and lysed in 100 μL RIPA buffer (50 mM Tris-HCl (pH 7.5), 150 mM NaCl, 1% (vol/vol) Triton X-100, 0.1% SDS, 0.5% deoxycholate, 1 mM PMSF, Protease Inhibitor Cocktail (Thermo Fisher Scientific)) supplemented with quenchers (10 mM sodium azide, 10 mM sodium ascorbate and 5 mM Trolox) for 20 min on ice. To ensure maximum recovery of biotinylated proteins, excess BP was removed by filtering the post-nuclear lysates using 3K MWCO protein concentrator columns (Pierce, Thermo Fisher Scientific). Biotinylated proteins were then enriched using 20 µL streptavidin magnetic beads (Pierce, Thermo Fisher Scientific) per sample at 4°C overnight. The bead-bound proteins were then washed with a series of buffers (2x RIPA, 1x 1 M KCl, 1x 0.1 M Na2CO3, 1x 2 M urea in 10 mM Tris-HCl, pH 8.0, 2x RIPA) and either subjected to down-stream MS or analyzed by western blot. Streptavidin magnetic beads containing bead-bound proteins were washed and stored in 100 µL 100 mM Triethylammoniumbicarbonat buffer (TEAB). For that, proteins were digested on-bead using trypsin. Proteins were reduced for 1 hour at 55°C using a final concentration of 5 mM Tris(2-carboxyethyl) phosphin (TCEP) followed by reduction for 30 min at room temperature using a final concentration of 10 mM methyl methanethiosulfonate (MMTS). Trypsin was added in a ratio of 1 µg trypsin to 30 µg protein, and proteins were digested overnight at 37°C while shaking. Beads were spun down, and supernatants were transferred to LowBind Eppendorf tubes. Beads were resuspended in 50 µL 250 mM TEAB, spun down, and supernatants were combined and vacuum dried. Pellets were suspended in 50 µL 250 mM TEAB containing 5 mM TCEP and 10 mM MMTS and sonicated for 10 min in an ultrasound water bath. For peptide clean-up, an adapted SP3 protocol was applied. For that, 20 µL carboxylate beads were added, and peptides were allowed to bind overnight upon acetonitrile (ACN) addition to a final concentration of at least 95%. Beads were spun down, supernatants were transferred to new LowBind Eppendorf tubes, and 20 µL carboxylate beads were added to increase peptide recovery. Then, beads were combined and washed twice with 100% ACN. Peptides were eluted by using first 20 µL 2% DMSO, followed by a second step with 20 µL Millipore H2O. Purified peptides were vacuum dried, and pellets were suspended in 40 µL 0.1% formic acid (FA) and transferred to HPLC vials.\n'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
